    {
      "topLeftX": 182,
      "topLeftY": 310,
      "bottomRightX": 606,
      "bottomRightY": 344,
      "content": "You'll find the following. When a photon comes in, you always find that only one of the four counters goes off.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 182,
      "topLeftY": 349,
      "bottomRightX": 607,
      "bottomRightY": 413,
      "content": "If the photon is $O$ from the first calcite, then the second calcite gives $O-O$ with probability $\\cos^2\\phi$ or $O-E$ with the complementary probability $1-\\cos^1\\phi=\\sin^2\\phi$. Likewise an $E$ photon gives a $E-O$ with the probability $\\sin^2\\phi$ or an $E-E$ with the probability $\\cos^2\\phi$.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 240,
      "topLeftY": 435,
      "bottomRightX": 547,
      "bottomRightY": 451,
      "content": "### 8. TWO-PHOTON CORRELATION EXPERIMENT",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "title"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 182,
      "topLeftY": 467,
      "bottomRightX": 604,
      "bottomRightY": 497,
      "content": "Let us turn now to the two photon correlation experiment (see Figure 4).",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 182,
      "topLeftY": 498,
      "bottomRightX": 606,
      "bottomRightY": 577,
      "content": "What can happen is that an atom emits two photons in opposite direction (e.g., the $3s \\to 2p \\to 1s$ transition in the H atom). They are observed simultaneously (say, by you and by me) through two calcites set at $\\phi_1$ and $\\phi_2$ to the vertical. Quantum theory and experiment agree that the probability $P_{OO}$ that both of us detect an ordinary photon is",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 330,
      "topLeftY": 588,
      "bottomRightX": 454,
      "bottomRightY": 608,
      "content": "$$P_{OO} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos^2(\\phi_2 - \\phi_1)$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 182,
      "topLeftY": 620,
      "bottomRightX": 604,
      "bottomRightY": 637,
      "content": "The probability $P_{EE}$ that we both observe an extraordinary ray is the same",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 330,
      "topLeftY": 648,
      "bottomRightX": 454,
      "bottomRightY": 668,
      "content": "$$P_{EE} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos^2(\\phi_2 - \\phi_1)$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 182,
      "topLeftY": 680,
      "bottomRightX": 473,
      "bottomRightY": 697,
      "content": "The probability $P_{OE}$ that I find $O$ and you find $E$ is",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 331,
      "topLeftY": 709,
      "bottomRightX": 451,
      "bottomRightY": 729,
      "content": "$$P_{OE} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin^2(\\phi_2 - \\phi_1)$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
